# LaTeX Engineering

Distilled from: X-LANCE Lab pre-print template (the upstream of `template/amphion.cls`), Overleaf "Management in a large project", thetapad.com large LaTeX projects, dmsussman.org LaTeX collaboration guide.

## Document class

- Document class is `\documentclass{template/amphion}`. The class lives under `template/` together with `asr-macros.sty`, `IEEEtran2.bst`, `venues.bib` and `assets/logos/`. Each report's `latexmkrc` puts the repository root on `TEXINPUTS` so these resolve.
- The default theme is warm ivory with petrol teal. Options: `opendfm` and `xlance` switch theme, `withlogo` restores the first-page logos (off by default), `notitlebox` removes the title box.
- The class already loads, in this order: `geometry, microtype, placeins, url, hyphenat, setspace, parskip, babel, lipsum, etoolbox, fontenc, tgpagella, mathpazo, inconsolata, amsmath, amssymb, mathtools, fancyhdr, graphicx, subcaption, float, booktabs, nicematrix, multirow, bm, longtable, tabularx, array, adjustbox, xspace, enumitem, tcolorbox, xcolor, listings, hyperref, cleveref, natbib, tikz`. Do **not** re-`\usepackage` any of these in `main.tex` -- it can trigger Option clash.
- The class redefines `\title`, `\author[N]{...}`, `\affiliation[N]{...}`, `\contribution{...}`, `\abstract{...}`, `\metadatainfo{...}`, `\publishdate`, `\revisedate`, `\maketitle`. `\equalcontribution{Name}` and `\correspondence{email}{Name}` are protected commands meant to be wrapped around an `\author{}` argument.
- `\abstract{...}` is a **command, not an environment**. The body lives in `sections/00_abstract.tex` as plain prose; `main.tex` consumes it via `\abstract{\input{sections/00_abstract}}` in the preamble.
- A report may open with a scenario or teaser figure. Refer back to a figure with `\Cref` rather than embedding it twice.

## File layout

- `main.tex` is orchestration only: documentclass, preamble overrides, title/author/abstract, `\input{sections/...}`, bibliography, appendix. No prose body in `main.tex`.
- `sections/XX_name.tex` -- one section per file, prefixed by 2-digit order.
- `figures/<name>.tex` -- TikZ source. Build with `cd figures && TEXINPUTS=../../../figures//: pdflatex <name>.tex`; the resulting `figures/<name>.pdf` is checked in.
- `figures/<name>.svg` -- SVG source for illustrations (scenes, people, devices) that are awkward in TikZ. Use the palette HEX colours and `font-family="TeX Gyre Pagella"`, convert with `tools/svg2pdf.sh`, and check in both the SVG and the resulting `figures/<name>.pdf`.
- `references.bib` -- the report's own bibliography. Combined with `template/venues.bib` at compile time via `\bibliography{template/venues, references}`.
- `template/` -- shared by every report; change it only through the public-layer process (AGENTS.md rule 8).
- Tables that exceed ~30 rows or are script-generated: separate file under `tables/`, `\input` from the relevant section.

## Figure claim and readability gate

- A teaser figure must visually communicate the paper's one ping and every headline capability it claims. Do not omit a capability from the graphic and repair the omission with a caption sentence.
- Architecture figures must show the actual semantic data flow, including all branches that affect the claim. For contextual ASR, show candidate retrieval, hotword text tokenisation/encoding, context injection, and the decoder path; do not collapse retrieval and conditioning into one ambiguous "RAG" arrow.
- Label inherited components and owned components distinctly. Cite the source of a borrowed named component at its first semantic mention, including a figure caption when the caption is the first mention.
- Inspect the rendered PDF at 100% scale. Figure body text should be approximately the paper body size, and no critical label should depend on zooming. Prefer fewer, larger panels over dense dashboard-style miniatures.
- A figure caption should explain the takeaway and reading order, not introduce a missing method or claim that the figure itself does not show.

## When editing sections

- Prefer `\input{sections/XX_name}` over `\include{}`.
- Use `\includeonly{sections/05_training}` in `main.tex` only as a temporary local edit when iterating fast on one section. Remove before commit.
- Cross-reference: always use `\label{type:name}` with one of these prefixes -- `sec:`, `subsec:`, `fig:`, `tab:`, `eq:`, `app:`, `alg:`. Reference with `\Cref` (cleveref capitalised; produces "Section 4" / "Figure 1" automatically).
- Math: define recurring symbols once in `main.tex` preamble with `\newcommand`. Examples already defined: `\wer`, `\cer`, `\mer`, `\hotwerN`. Do not redefine in sections.
- Code listings: use one of the four styles shipped by `template/amphion.cls` -- `python`, `json`, `bash`, `plain`. For boxed text use the `promptbox` tcolorbox environment, and for note callouts `notebox`. Literal prompts do not go in a report (AGENTS.md rule 4).

## Bibliography rules (IEEE numeric)

- Citation format is IEEE numeric `[1]` via `natbib + template/IEEEtran2.bst` with `\setcitestyle{numbers}`.
- Use `\cite{key}` for all citations (parenthetical and inline). The numeric back-end ignores the `\citep / \citet` distinction. Multi-cites: `\cite{a, b, c}` not `\cite{a}\cite{b}`.
- BibTeX key format: `<lastname><year><firstcontentword>`, lowercase, no punctuation. Example: `gulati2020conformer`, `radford2023whisper`. Existing keys must not be renamed (cross-section refs would break silently).
- Every new cite must include: author (full names with `and`), year, title, booktitle / journal, volume / number / pages where applicable. Prefer the published-venue entry over arXiv when both exist; if both are needed, keep the published one and omit the other.
- `template/venues.bib` holds conference and journal name strings inherited from the upstream template. Do not add custom keys there; put new entries in the report's `references.bib` and rely on `template/venues.bib` only when its `@string` macros help canonicalise venue names.
- After adding a cite, run a build right away to make sure `bibtex` resolves it.

## Numbers, units, and typography

- Thousand separator inside math: `10{,}000` (the `{}` keeps the comma upright).
- Numbers and units always glued with a thin space: `960\,h`, `16\,kHz`, `7\,B`, `5e-7` is fine in tables.
- Percent sign: `5\%` (escape the `%`).
- Em-dash for parenthetical breaks: `---` (LaTeX renders as one character). Do not use a hyphen.
- Quotes: ``  `single' `` and `` ``double'' ``. Never `"..."`.
- Algorithm-style code: use `lstlisting` with `style=plain` (or `python` / `json` / `bash` for typed code). Do not `\verb|...|` for multi-line content.
- Convenience commands shipped by `template/amphion.cls`: `\eg`, `\ie`. Use them for "e.g." and "i.e." where appropriate (renders italic with the trailing comma + thin space already attached).

## Compile workflow

- Default, from `egs/<slug>/`: `latexmk -pdf -interaction=nonstopmode main.tex`
- After every substantive content edit, run `latexmk` and confirm exit code 0 before reporting completion.
- If a fresh package is needed: `tlmgr install <pkg>`. Add it to the `tlmgr install` line in `README.md` so a fresh checkout reproduces.
- Do not commit `main.pdf` (`.gitignore` already enforces). Do commit `figures/*.pdf` for reproducibility.

## What never to do

- Do not silently change the document class, theme option, or geometry; these affect reviewer page count and headers.
- Do not re-`\usepackage` anything that `template/amphion.cls` already loads (see the list above) -- it produces an `Option clash` error.
- Do not introduce a package whose macros conflict with the class's redefinitions of `\section`, `\maketitle`, `\abstract`, `\title`, `\author`, `\affiliation`. If unsure, build first and inspect the log.
- Do not use Unicode characters in source where a LaTeX command exists (use `\alpha` not `α`, `\textendash` not `--` if you specifically want an en-dash).
- Do not edit files inside `template/` from a report commit. Override behaviour in that report's `main.tex` preamble, or change the template through the public-layer process.
